---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ArticleFeedback from "../../components/ArticleFeedback.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
    title="How to Take Good Dating Profile Photos When You Hate Being in Pictures"
    description="How to take better dating profile photos when you feel awkward, uncomfortable, or simply dislike being photographed."
>
    <main class="article-page">
        <article>
            <header class="article-header">
                <p class="eyebrow">DATING PROFILE PHOTOS</p>

                <h1>
                    How to Take Good Dating Profile Photos When You Hate Being
                    in Pictures
                </h1>

                <p class="article-intro">
                    How to take better dating profile photos when you feel
                    awkward, uncomfortable, or simply dislike being
                    photographed.
                </p>
            </header>

            <div class="article-content">
                <p>
                    There is a particular kind of frustration that happens when
                    you need photos of yourself.
                </p>

                <p>You know you need a few good ones.</p>

                <p>Maybe you're updating a dating profile.</p>

                <p>Maybe your current photos are old.</p>

                <p>
                    Maybe you've been using the same two pictures for longer
                    than you'd like to admit.
                </p>

                <p>So you decide to take some new ones.</p>

                <p>And almost immediately, you regret the decision.</p>

                <p>You don't know where to stand.</p>

                <p>You don't know what to do with your hands.</p>

                <p>Your smile feels forced.</p>

                <p>You look at the first photo and think you look strange.</p>

                <p>You take another.</p>

                <p>Then another.</p>

                <p>Somehow they all look worse.</p>

                <p>
                    If you hate being photographed, the problem may not be that
                    you are bad at taking pictures.
                </p>

                <p>
                    You may simply be trying to do something that feels
                    unnatural in the first place.
                </p>

                <h2>Being photographed is a strange thing to have to do</h2>

                <p>
                    Most of the time, you don't think about what your face is
                    doing.
                </p>

                <p>You walk somewhere.</p>

                <p>You talk to someone.</p>

                <p>You make coffee.</p>

                <p>You laugh at something.</p>

                <p>You look out a window.</p>

                <p>Your body just moves.</p>

                <p>Then someone points a camera at you.</p>

                <p>Now suddenly you're aware of your face.</p>

                <p>Your posture.</p>

                <p>Your hands.</p>

                <p>Even the way you're standing.</p>

                <p>
                    Things you normally never think about become things you have
                    to think about.
                </p>

                <p>And that can make almost anyone look less natural.</p>

                <p>You are no longer simply doing something.</p>

                <p>You're doing something while watching yourself do it.</p>

                <h2>Don't start by trying to look good</h2>

                <p>
                    This sounds backwards when you're taking dating profile
                    photos.
                </p>

                <p>Of course you want to look good.</p>

                <p>
                    But if that's the only thing you're thinking about, the
                    camera can turn into a test.
                </p>

                <p>You check your face.</p>

                <p>Then your hair.</p>

                <p>Then your smile.</p>

                <p>Then the angle.</p>

                <p>Then you take another photo to fix the previous one.</p>

                <p>Before long, you're not really taking photos anymore.</p>

                <p>You're inspecting yourself.</p>

                <p>That usually makes the process harder.</p>

                <p>
                    A better starting point is to give yourself something to do.
                </p>

                <p>Walk.</p>

                <p>Make coffee.</p>

                <p>Look through a book.</p>

                <p>Fix your hair.</p>

                <p>Put something in a bag.</p>

                <p>Sit outside.</p>

                <p>Do something that gives your body a reason to move.</p>

                <p>You don't have to pretend the camera isn't there.</p>

                <p>
                    You just don't have to make the camera your entire activity.
                </p>

                <h2>Movement gives you somewhere to go</h2>

                <p>
                    Standing still and trying to look natural is surprisingly
                    difficult.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Your body doesn't have much information about what should
                    happen next.
                </p>

                <p>So you start controlling it.</p>

                <p>Should I move my hand?</p>

                <p>Should I smile?</p>

                <p>Where should I look?</p>

                <p>Is this normal?</p>

                <p>
                    You have never thought about your hands this much in your
                    life.
                </p>

                <p>Movement changes that.</p>

                <p>A small action gives your body a reason to be somewhere.</p>

                <p>Walking changes your posture naturally.</p>

                <p>Holding a coffee gives your hands something to do.</p>

                <p>Looking through a store gives your eyes somewhere to go.</p>

                <p>
                    Turning toward someone changes your expression without you
                    having to create one.
                </p>

                <p>The goal isn't to create a dramatic candid moment.</p>

                <p>
                    It's simply to stop asking your body to perform “being
                    natural.”
                </p>

                <h2>You don't need someone who is good at photography</h2>

                <p>
                    It helps if the person taking your photos knows what they're
                    doing.
                </p>

                <p>
                    But you don't necessarily need a professional photographer.
                </p>

                <p>
                    You also don't need someone standing there giving you
                    complicated posing instructions.
                </p>

                <p>In fact, too much direction can make the problem worse.</p>

                <p>If someone keeps saying:</p>

                <blockquote>
                    <p>“Smile.”</p>

                    <p>“Turn your head.”</p>

                    <p>“Relax.”</p>

                    <p>“Look natural.”</p>
                </blockquote>

                <p>you now have four more things to think about.</p>

                <p>
                    A better photo often comes from doing something ordinary
                    while someone takes several pictures.
                </p>

                <p>The useful photograph may happen somewhere in the middle.</p>

                <p>Not when you're trying hardest.</p>

                <h2>Take more photos than you think you need</h2>

                <p>One photograph puts a lot of pressure on the moment.</p>

                <p>Twenty photographs give the moment somewhere to go.</p>

                <p>The first few may feel awkward.</p>

                <p>Then you stop noticing the camera quite as much.</p>

                <p>Your expression changes.</p>

                <p>Your posture changes.</p>

                <p>You start moving normally again.</p>

                <p>
                    This is one reason continuous shooting or short bursts can
                    be useful.
                </p>

                <p>You aren't trying to create one perfect pose.</p>

                <p>
                    You're giving yourself enough time for the awkwardness to
                    pass.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The best picture may be the one taken a second before you
                    realize someone is still taking photos.
                </p>

                <h2>Don't judge the photos immediately</h2>

                <p>This part is easy to underestimate.</p>

                <p>You take a picture.</p>

                <p>You look at it.</p>

                <p>You dislike your face.</p>

                <p>So you change what you're doing.</p>

                <p>Then you take another.</p>

                <p>
                    Now you're trying to correct the previous photograph instead
                    of simply taking the next one.
                </p>

                <p>It can become a strange feedback loop.</p>

                <p>Photo.</p>

                <p>Judgment.</p>

                <p>Adjustment.</p>

                <p>Photo.</p>

                <p>Judgment.</p>

                <p>Adjustment.</p>

                <p>
                    At some point, you're not responding to the moment anymore.
                </p>

                <p>You're responding to your own reflection.</p>

                <p>If you can, take a group of photos first.</p>

                <p>Look at them later.</p>

                <p>
                    It's much easier to notice which ones actually feel natural
                    when you aren't trying to fix your face between every shot.
                </p>

                <h2>The best photo may not be your favorite photo</h2>

                <p>This is particularly important for dating profiles.</p>

                <p>
                    Your favorite photograph is often the one where you think
                    you look the most attractive.
                </p>

                <p>That's understandable.</p>

                <p>
                    But the strongest profile photo may be the one where you
                    look the most like a person.
                </p>

                <p>Maybe your smile isn't perfectly symmetrical.</p>

                <p>Maybe your hair isn't sitting exactly right.</p>

                <p>Maybe you're looking slightly away from the camera.</p>

                <p>Maybe you're caught in the middle of something.</p>

                <p>That doesn't automatically make the photograph worse.</p>

                <p>It may make it feel less constructed.</p>

                <p>
                    And that's useful when someone is trying to get a sense of
                    what you might actually be like.
                </p>

                <h2>You don't have to become someone who loves photos</h2>

                <p>
                    There is another expectation that can make this harder than
                    it needs to be.
                </p>

                <p>
                    You might think the solution is to become comfortable in
                    front of the camera.
                </p>

                <p>Maybe eventually you will.</p>

                <p>But you don't need to enjoy having your picture taken.</p>

                <p>You only need to make the process less unnatural.</p>

                <p>Those are different things.</p>

                <p>
                    Someone can dislike being photographed and still have good
                    photographs.
                </p>

                <p>
                    They can feel awkward while the camera is there and still
                    look relaxed in the final image.
                </p>

                <p>
                    They can prefer being behind the camera and still have a
                    dating profile that feels warm and believable.
                </p>

                <p>You don't need to change your personality.</p>

                <p>
                    You just need to make the situation easier for your normal
                    behavior to come through.
                </p>

                <h2>Think about the whole profile, not one photograph</h2>

                <p>A dating profile doesn't depend on one perfect picture.</p>

                <p>One photograph can show your face clearly.</p>

                <p>Another can show you doing something you enjoy.</p>

                <p>Another can show a different expression or setting.</p>

                <p>
                    Together, they give someone more information than any single
                    image could.
                </p>

                <p>
                    This also means you don't need every photograph to carry the
                    same pressure.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If one photo is your clearest face shot, another can simply
                    show a different part of your life.
                </p>

                <p>That gives you more freedom when taking them.</p>

                <p>You aren't trying to make every frame perfect.</p>

                <p>You're building a small collection of believable moments.</p>

                <h2>
                    If you hate being photographed, make the situation less
                    photographic
                </h2>

                <p>This is probably the simplest change.</p>

                <p>
                    Don't stand in front of a camera and wait for yourself to
                    suddenly become comfortable.
                </p>

                <p>Give yourself a reason to move.</p>

                <p>Give yourself something to look at.</p>

                <p>Give your hands something to do.</p>

                <p>
                    Let someone take several photographs instead of waiting for
                    one perfect moment.
                </p>

                <p>
                    And give yourself some time before deciding whether the
                    photos are good.
                </p>

                <p>The camera doesn't need to become invisible.</p>

                <p>It just needs to become less important.</p>

                <p>
                    Because if you already dislike being photographed, making
                    the photograph itself the main event probably isn't going to
                    help.
                </p>

                <p>
                    A better photo may happen when you're busy doing something
                    else.
                </p>

                <p>Not because you're pretending the camera isn't there.</p>

                <p>
                    Because, for a moment, you have something more interesting
                    to think about.
                </p>
            </div>

            <ArticleFeedback
                article="how-to-take-good-dating-profile-photos-when-you-hate-being-in-pictures"
            />
        </article>
    </main>
</BaseLayout>

